    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Since the late 1970s, China has grown from a poor but nominally egalitarian country into a wealthy one with stark divides. In recent years, China's economic growth has slowed down, and there are fears for some that it will soon reverse. Using survey data from 2004–2023, this thesis examines how demographic factors and economic conditions have shaped changing attitudes toward inequality among ordinary citizens in China. In particular, viewpoints toward “why the rich are rich” and “why the poor are poor” have shifted from focusing on individual, meritocratic factors to systemic, external ones. Further analysis reveals the emergence of an attributional divide along generational and socioeconomic lines, with older, more-educated, and relatively affluent respondents more likely to emphasize individual explanations for economic outcomes than their younger, less-educated, and less privileged counterparts. Given recent efforts to re-emphasize the reducing of inequality as a key policy objective, this combination of slowing economic growth and increasing dissatisfaction may present a challenge for China's leadership as they seek to reinvigorate the economy and maintain public legitimacy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Alisky, M. (2024). Common Prosperity and China's Social Volcano: Evaluating Changing Attitudes Toward Inequality. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/qd401db6968. https://doi.org/10.25740/qd401db6968.",
